{"timestamp":"2026-11-16T15:15:42.690Z","ordinal":3108,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d516-ad7f-7071-81ac-409a5dad90b1","turn_id":"01a1e4cf-42fc-7bf1-b6f5-3fd32ae90631","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-ebd2cfbc-6b63-454b-86f6-94d27aef2464","process_id":"68025","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","python3 -c 'import sys;open(sys.argv[1],\"ab\").write(sys.argv[2].encode())' '/Users/puneetpalsingh/.codex/entire-20-audit-2026-11-16/public/git-sync-108.json' '{\n  \"p\": {\n    \"url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/entireio/git-sync/pulls/108\",\n    \"id\": 4314033357,\n    \"node_id\": \"PR_kwDOR9SkY88AAAABASLszQ\",\n    \"html_url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108\",\n    \"diff_url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108.diff\",\n    \"patch_url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108.patch\",\n    \"issue_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/entireio/git-sync/issues/108\",\n    \"commits_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/entireio/git-sync/pulls/108/commits\",\n    \"review_comments_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/entireio/git-sync/pulls/108/comments\",\n    \"review_comment_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/entireio/git-sync/pulls/comments{/number}\",\n    \"comments_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/entireio/git-sync/issues/108/comments\",\n    \"statuses_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/entireio/git-sync/statuses/503ac1ba90d9be140bf6c4c348cc0b5b249eaf5d\",\n    \"number\": 108,\n    \"state\": \"closed\",\n    \"locked\": false,\n    \"title\": \"Bind explicit credentials to the host they were configured for\",\n    \"user\": {\n      \"login\": \"Soph\",\n      \"id\": 70741,\n      \"node_id\": \"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzQx\",\n      \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?v=4\",\n      \"gravatar_id\": \"\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/Soph\",\n      \"html_url\": \"https://github.com/Soph\",\n      \"followers_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/Soph/followers\",\n      \"following_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/Soph/following{/other_user}\",\n      \"gists_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/Soph/gists{/gist_id}\",\n      \"starred_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/Soph/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\n      \"subscriptions_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/Soph/subscriptions\",\n      \"organizations_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/Soph/orgs\",\n      \"repos_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/Soph/repos\",\n      \"events_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/Soph/events{/privacy}\",\n      \"received_events_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/Soph/received_events\",\n      \"type\": \"User\",\n      \"user_view_type\": \"public\",\n      \"site_admin\": false\n    },\n    \"body\": \"Fixes the highest-severity item left from the security review. **Stacked on #107** so the `smarthttp.go` changes don'\"'\"'t conflict — the base is that branch, so this diff shows only its own changes. I'\"'\"'ll rebase onto `main` once #107 merges.\\n\\n## The problem\\n\\nWith `--source-follow-info-refs-redirect` / `--target-follow-info-refs-redirect`, git-sync adopts the host `/info/refs` redirected to and addresses it directly for follow-up RPCs — re-attaching the configured token to requests aimed at that host, *after* Go'\"'\"'s `http.Client` had deliberately stripped `Authorization` on the cross-host hop.\\n\\nReproduced with two local servers, where the endpoint 307s to an unrelated host:\\n\\n```\\n[LEGIT] GET  Authorization=\\\"Basic Z2l0OlNVUEVSX1NFQ1JFVF9QQVRfMTIzNDU=\\\"\\n[EVIL]  GET  Authorization=\\\"\\\"                    ← stdlib stripped it\\n[EVIL]  POST Authorization=\\\"Basic Z2l0OlNVUEVS…\\\" ← git-sync re-sent it\\n```\\n\\nAny redirect an attacker can cause is enough: an open redirect on the real host, a hostile mirror, or a MITM on a plain-`http` source. On the target side the token usually carries write access.\\n\\n## The fix\\n\\nAn explicitly supplied credential — token flags, bearer flags, or userinfo in the URL — now goes only to the host named on the command line and its subdomains, on the same scheme and port. Anything else gets nothing, plus a warning naming the withheld host, and the credential helper is consulted for that host instead:\\n\\n```\\nsource: withholding the configured credentials from http://localhost:64772/repo.git:\\n/info/refs redirected outside 127.0.0.1. Consulting the git credential helper for that host instead.\\n```\\n\\nCredentials resolved *from* the helper are untouched — they'\"'\"'re looked up keyed on the host being challenged, so they were already bound to their destination. The subdomain allowance keeps the case the flag exists for working: `example.com` → `replica.example.com` still authenticates.\\n\\n## Why redirect policy moved into git-sync\\n\\nThe same-site rule is stricter than the stdlib'\"'\"'s on purpose. Go compares hostnames only, ignoring scheme and port, so it forwards `Authorization` from `host:443` to `host:9999` — a different service, potentially a different tenant. My first version of the end-to-end test caught exactly this: both `httptest` servers are on `127.0.0.1`, so the stdlib happily carried the token across the port boundary and the test failed.\\n\\nThat hop isn'\"'\"'t visible from the request-building code, because the redirect is followed *inside* a single `http.Client.Do`. So policy now lives in a per-connection `CheckRedirect` that strips the header on any hop leaving the endpoint'\"'\"'s site, layered over whatever policy the caller set (theirs still runs and can still veto). Setting `CheckRedirect` replaces the stdlib'\"'\"'s ten-redirect default, so that cap is reimposed explicitly — otherwise this fix would have introduced a redirect-loop hang.\\n\\nThe client is shallow-copied per connection, sharing the caller'\"'\"'s `Transport`, so nothing the caller passed in is mutated or reconfigured.\\n\\n## Tests\\n\\n- `sameSite` in isolation, including the near-misses: suffix without a dot boundary (`notgithub.com` vs `github.com`), attacker-controlled prefix domain (`github.com.evil.example`), scheme downgrade, port change, and IP addresses having no subdomains.\\n- End to end against `httptest`: the cross-site POST must carry no credentials, and a same-site redirect must still carry them.\\n- By hand: the original two-server reproduction now logs an empty `Authorization` on both requests to the redirect target.\\n\\n`gofmt`, `go vet`, `golangci-lint` (0 issues), `go test ./...` 17/17, `-race` on `gitproto` and `syncer`.\\n\\n## Behavior change to be aware of\\n\\nIf a deployment today relies on the redirect target receiving the configured token and that target is **not** same-site with the endpoint, it will start getting 401s. The fix is to store those credentials in the git credential helper against the redirect host — git-sync will find them there, and the helper is now installed alongside an explicit token so that path actually works.\\n\\nWhat counts as same-site, precisely:\\n\\n| redirect | credentials |\\n|---|---|\\n| same host, any path | kept |\\n| subdomain of the endpoint host | kept |\\n| `http` → `https`, same host | kept (upgrade) |\\n| `https` → `http` | **withheld** (downgrade) |\\n| different host | **withheld** |\\n| different port (default ports normalized) | **withheld** |\\n\\n`docs/usage.md` gains a \\\"Credentials and redirects\\\" section explaining this; the flags were previously documented only in their flag help text.\\n\\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\\n\\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\\n---\\n\\n> [!NOTE]\\n> **High Risk**\\n> Changes authentication and credential routing on HTTP redirects—a security-critical path—with behavior change for deployments that relied on sending configured tokens to non-same-site redirect hosts.\\n> \\n> **Overview**\\n> Fixes a **credential leak** when `--source-follow-info-refs-redirect` / `--target-follow-info-refs-redirect` is enabled: after `/info/refs` redirects, follow-up RPCs hit the redirect host directly and git-sync was re-attaching CLI/URL tokens that Go’s client had already stripped on the cross-host hop.\\n> \\n> **Explicit credentials** (tokens, bearer flags, URL userinfo) are now sent only to the configured host’s **same site** — matching host or subdomain, **same scheme and port** — via new `sameSite` logic and `outgoingAuth` on `HTTPConn`. Off-site redirect targets get no token, a one-time stderr warning, and the git credential helper on 401. Helper-resolved creds are unchanged.\\n> \\n> Redirect handling adds **`guardRedirects`**: a shallow `http.Client` copy with `CheckRedirect` that drops `Authorization` on hops leaving the endpoint site (tighter than stdlib hostname-only rules) and re-applies the 10-redirect cap. Info/refs and RPC paths use `outgoingAuth` instead of raw `c.Auth`; helper attach paths mark `authIsExplicit = false`.\\n> \\n> Docs add **Credentials and redirects** in `usage.md`; CHANGELOG documents the security fix. Tests cover `sameSite` edge cases and end-to-end cross-site vs same-site redirect behavior.\\n> \\n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit b0e0f88541d3bbbe472233a7ce0bbe63124c4115. Configure [here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\\n\",\n    \"labels\": [],\n    \"milestone\": null,\n    \"active_lock_reason\": null,\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T13:09:08Z\",\n    \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-20T15:36:34Z\",\n    \"closed_at\": \"2026-08-20T15:36:30Z\",\n    \"merged_at\": \"2026-08-20T15:36:30Z\",\n    \"merge_commit_sha\": \"4e816de76249ee4a91659879d57fa0e0fe9ae240\",\n    \"assignee\": null,\n    \"assignees\": [],\n    \"requested_reviewers\": [],\n    \"requested_teams\": [],\n    \"head\": {\n      \"label\": \"entireio:soph/security-bind-token-to-host\",\n      \"ref\": \"soph/security-bind-token-to-host\",\n      \"repo\": {\n        \"id\": 1205118