{"timestamp":"2026-11-16T15:15:43.046Z","ordinal":3111,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d516-ad7f-7071-81ac-409a5dad90b1","turn_id":"01a1e4cf-42fc-7bf1-b6f5-3fd32ae90631","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-8a1f597c-e28f-4384-af2d-f8e9727a7520","process_id":"47404","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","python3 -c 'import sys;open(sys.argv[1],\"ab\").write(sys.argv[2].encode())' '/Users/puneetpalsingh/.codex/entire-20-audit-2026-11-16/public/git-sync-108.json' 'REDACTED\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\"><themed-picture data-catalyst-inline=\\\"true\\\"><picture><source media=\\\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\\\" srcset=\\\"https://camo.githubusercontent.com/a3745bd9c9e494e3d083228c1efdaf4ec2d519ad1c6adb4160d1c56c1ddeeb7b/68747470733a2f2f637572736f722e636f6d2f6173736574732f696d616765732f6669782d696e2d637572736f722d6461726b2e706e67\\\" data-canonical-src=\\\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/fix-in-cursor-dark.png\\\"><source media=\\\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\\\" srcset=\\\"https://camo.githubusercontent.com/8f892a2b1c7e36d49621daf12d83d3200c0ad6842ca1f6d8422631d06c99b3b2/68747470733a2f2f637572736f722e636f6d2f6173736574732f696d616765732f6669782d696e2d637572736f722d6c696768742e706e67\\\" data-canonical-src=\\\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/fix-in-cursor-light.png\\\"><img alt=\\\"Fix All in Cursor\\\" width=\\\"115\\\" height=\\\"28\\\" src=\\\"https://camo.githubusercontent.com/a3745bd9c9e494e3d083228c1efdaf4ec2d519ad1c6adb4160d1c56c1ddeeb7b/68747470733a2f2f637572736f722e636f6d2f6173736574732f696d616765732f6669782d696e2d637572736f722d6461726b2e706e67\\\" data-canonical-src=\\\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/fix-in-cursor-dark.png\\\"></picture></themed-picture></a></p>\\n\\n\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><sup>❌ Bugbot Autofix is OFF. To automatically fix reported issues with cloud agents, enable autofix in the <a href=\\\"https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">Cursor dashboard</a>.</sup></p>\\n\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><em>Comment <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">@cursor review</code> or <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">bugbot run</code> to trigger another review on this PR</em></p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><sup>Reviewed by <a href=\\\"https://cursor.com/bugbot\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">Cursor Bugbot</a> for commit <a class=\\\"commit-link\\\" data-hovercard-type=\\\"commit\\\" data-hovercard-url=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/b0e0f88541d3bbbe472233a7ce0bbe63124c4115/hovercard\\\" href=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/b0e0f88541d3bbbe472233a7ce0bbe63124c4115\\\"><tt>b0e0f88</tt></a>. Configure <a href=\\\"https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">here</a>.</sup></p>\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#discussion_r3813653044\",\n        \"body\": \"Confirmed and fixed in d3eb5b95 — and it was worse than the flag-token case, because it was **silent**.\\n\\nReproduced against httptest servers before fixing: with credentials only in the URL and no explicit `Auth`, the cross-site POST carried `Basic dXNlcjpVUkxTRUNSRVQ=` (`user:URLSECRET`). You had the mechanism exactly right — `resolvedEndpoint` was `*c.EndpointURL` with only scheme and host replaced, so userinfo came along, and net/http derives Basic auth from `req.URL.User`. `outgoingAuth` never sees that, so there was no warning either.\\n\\nBoth construction sites now go through one `setResolvedEndpoint` helper that drops userinfo when the result leaves the endpoint'\"'\"'s site, and warns when it does. Verified against the built binary: the redirect target now logs `Authorization=\\\"\\\"` and the operator gets the withheld-credentials warning.\\n\\nGood catch on the docs too — the claim that userinfo is bound like a flag token was aspirational when you flagged it, and is now true.\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 3813653044,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": 4972971969,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": 3813268165,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:00:59Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:00:59Z\",\n        \"review\": null,\n        \"side\": \"RIGHT\",\n        \"line\": 409,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": \"internal/gitproto/smarthttp.go\",\n        \"body_html\": \"<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Confirmed and fixed in <a class=\\\"commit-link\\\" data-hovercard-type=\\\"commit\\\" data-hovercard-url=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853/hovercard\\\" href=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853\\\"><tt>d3eb5b9</tt></a> — and it was worse than the flag-token case, because it was <strong>silent</strong>.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Reproduced against httptest servers before fixing: with credentials only in the URL and no explicit <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">Auth</code>, the cross-site POST carried <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">Basic dXNlcjpVUkxTRUNSRVQ=</code> (<code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">user:URLSECRET</code>). You had the mechanism exactly right — <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">resolvedEndpoint</code> was <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">*c.EndpointURL</code> with only scheme and host replaced, so userinfo came along, and net/http derives Basic auth from <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">req.URL.User</code>. <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">outgoingAuth</code> never sees that, so there was no warning either.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Both construction sites now go through one <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">setResolvedEndpoint</code> helper that drops userinfo when the result leaves the endpoint'\"'\"'s site, and warns when it does. Verified against the built binary: the redirect target now logs <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">Authorization=\\\"\\\"</code> and the operator gets the withheld-credentials warning.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Good catch on the docs too — the claim that userinfo is bound like a flag token was aspirational when you flagged it, and is now true.</p>\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#pullrequestreview-4972971969\",\n        \"body\": \"\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?u=54538e7ba4f0714d98f7a9289c5b91400b262152&v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 4972971969,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": null,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": null,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:00:59Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": null,\n        \"review\": \"COMMENTED\",\n        \"side\": null,\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": null,\n        \"body_html\": \"\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#discussion_r3813653319\",\n        \"body\": \"Fixed in d3eb5b95. You'\"'\"'re right, and this one breaks working setups rather than leaking anything — a false negative.\\n\\n`sameSite` now normalizes the default port per scheme before comparing, so `https://host` and `https://host:443` are the same site, as are `http://host` and `http://host:80`. The default port for the *other* scheme still counts as a real port (`https://host` vs `https://host:80` stays cross-site), and the subdomain case with an explicit default port is covered too. Five new cases in the table.\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 3813653319,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": 4972972271,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": 3813268177,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:01Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:02Z\",\n        \"review\": null,\n        \"side\": \"RIGHT\",\n        \"line\": 52,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": \"internal/gitproto/samesite.go\",\n        \"body_html\": \"<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Fixed in <a class=\\\"commit-link\\\" data-hovercard-type=\\\"commit\\\" data-hovercard-url=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853/hovercard\\\" href=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853\\\"><tt>d3eb5b9</tt></a>. You'\"'\"'re right, and this one breaks working setups rather than leaking anything — a false negative.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">sameSite</code> now normalizes the default port per scheme before comparing, so <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https://host</code> and <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https://host:443</code> are the same site, as are <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http://host</code> and <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http://host:80</code>. The default port for the <em>other</em> scheme still counts as a real port (<code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https://host</code> vs <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https://host:80</code> stays cross-site), and the subdomain case with an explicit default port is covered too. Five new cases in the table.</p>\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#pullrequestreview-4972972271\",\n        \"body\": \"\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?u=54538e7ba4f0714d98f7a9289c5b91400b262152&v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 4972972271,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": null,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": null,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:01Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": null,\n        \"review\": \"COMMENTED\",\n        \"side\": null,\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": null,\n        \"body_html\": \"\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#discussion_r3813653514\",\n        \"body\": \"Fixed in d3eb5b95. Verified: `syncer.newConn` had `if authMethod == nil { conn.CredentialHelper = ... }`, which is exactly the case where the token is *not* withheld — so a `--source-token` user hitting a cross-site redirect got a bare 401, and the remedy `docs/usage.md` recommends did not work.\\n\\nThe helper is now installed alongside an explicit token. For same-site requests `outgoingAuth` still returns the token, so `tryHelperRetry` returns early and the helper is never consulted — it only comes into play where the token is deliberately withheld, which is the behaviour the warning and docs describe. The warning also no longer promises a lookup unconditionally: it names the real next step depending on whether a helper is configured.\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 3813653514,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": 4972972543,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": 3813268184,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:03Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:03Z\",\n        \"review\": null,\n        \"side\": \"RIGHT\",\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": \"internal/gitproto/smarthttp.go\",\n        \"body_html\": \"<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Fixed in <a class=\\\"commit-link\\\" data-hovercard-type=\\\"commit\\\" data-hovercard-url=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853/hovercard\\\" href=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853\\\"><tt>d3eb5b9</tt></a>. Verified: <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">syncer.newConn</code> had <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">if authMethod == nil { conn.CredentialHelper = ... }</code>, which is exactly the case where the token is <em>not</em> withheld — so a <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">--source-token</code> user hitting a cross-site redirect got a bare 401, and the remedy <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">docs/usage.md</code> recommends did not work.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">The helper is now installed alongside an explicit token. For same-site requests <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">outgoingAuth</code> still returns the token, so <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">tryHelperRetry</code> returns early and the helper is never consulted — it only comes into play where the token is deliberately withheld, which is the behaviour the warning and docs describe. The warning also no longer promises a lookup unconditionally: it names the real next step depending on whether a helper is configured.</p>\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#pullrequestreview-4972972543\",\n        \"body\": \"\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?u=54538e7ba4f0714d98f7a9289c5b91400b262152&v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 4972972543,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": null,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": null,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:03Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": null,\n        \"review\": \"COMMENTED\",\n        \"side\": null,\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": null,\n        \"body_html\": \"\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#pullrequestreview-4973565789\",\n        \"body\": \"Real bug, right fix, and the `sameSite` test table covers the cases that matter. `go vet` and `go test ./...` pass on the branch. Two things I'\"'\"'d change before merge — both are false *negatives* (credentials withheld where they shouldn'\"'\"'t be), so they break working setups rather than leak anything.\\n\\n## 1. `http` → `https` on the same host is now treated as cross-site\\n\\n`samesite.go:43` requires an exact scheme match, so `sameSite(http://host, https://host)` is `false` (verified directly). A plain-`http` endpoint that redirects to `https` on the same host — GitHub does this, and so does about every corporate reverse proxy — works today, because the stdlib carries `Authorization` when only the scheme changed. After this PR the token is stripped on that hop, and with `--follow-info-refs-redirect` on, `outgoingAuth` then withholds it from every follow-up RPC too. Result: a hard 401 on a config that works today, and the \\\"store it in the credential helper\\\" remedy doesn'\"'\"'t apply, because the host didn'\"'\"'t change.\\n\\nThe stated goal is \\\"an https endpoint never leaks a token over plaintext http\\\" — that'\"'\"'s the *downgrade* direction only. Suggest allowing the upgrade:\\n\\n```go\\nif !strings.EqualFold(orig.Scheme, dest.Scheme) &&\\n\\t!(strings.EqualFold(orig.Scheme, \\\"http\\\") && strings.EqualFold(dest.Scheme, \\\"https\\\")) {\\n\\treturn false\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe port normalization already handles 80 → 443. Either way this belongs in the \\\"Behavior change to be aware of\\\" section, which currently mentions only non-same-site *hosts*.\\n\\n## 2. `guardRedirects` anchors on the user-typed endpoint forever\\n\\n`smarthttp.go:363` closes over `EndpointURL` at construction, but the connection'\"'\"'s effective endpoint moves — `setResolvedEndpoint` adopts the redirect host, and helper credentials are then looked up *for that host*. Any further redirect that never leaves the adopted host still fails `sameSite(EndpointURL, req.URL)` and loses its `Authorization`. Reproduced: endpoint 307s to host X, helper creds bound to X, X bounces `POST /repo.git/...` → `/final/repo.git/...`:\\n\\n```\\nGET  /repo.git/info/refs              Authorization=\\\"\\\"\\nPOST /repo.git/git-upload-pack        Authorization=\\\"Basic Z2l0OkhFTFBFUkNSRUQ=\\\"\\nPOST /final/repo.git/git-upload-pack  Authorization=\\\"\\\"      ← stripped on a same-host hop\\n```\\n\\nThat'\"'\"'s exactly the path the PR'\"'\"'s own documented remedy sends people down, so it undercuts the fix. The credential is bound to the host the *request* was aimed at, which the callback already has: use `via[0].URL` instead of the captured `endpoint`. That'\"'\"'s per-request accurate for the token case (identical when `requestURL() == EndpointURL`) and for the helper-retry case (`via[0].URL` is the challenge URL), and it lets the closure drop the captured endpoint entirely.\\n\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"nodo\",\n          \"name\": \"nodo\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/829178?u=2f501cf3464df66b7ec3a6ab1eafc90d41b97974&v=4\",\n          \"id\": 829178\n        },\n        \"id\": 4973565789,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": null,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": null,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:59:14Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": null,\n        \"review\": \"COMMENTED\",\n        \"side\": null,\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": null,\n        \"body_html\": \"<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Real bug, right fix, and the <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">sameSite</code> test table covers the cases that matter. <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">go vet</code> and <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">go test ./...</code> pass on the branch. Two things I'\"'\"'d change before merge — both are false <em>negatives</em> (credentials withheld where they shouldn'\"'\"'t be), so they break working setups rather than leak anything.</p>\\n<h2 dir=\\\"auto\\\">1. <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http</code> → <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https</code> on the same host is now treated as cross-site</h2>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">samesite.go:43</code> requires an exact scheme match, so <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">sameSite(http://host, https://host)</code> is <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">false</code> (verified directly). A plain-<code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http</code> endpoint that redirects to <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https</code> on the same host — GitHub does this, and so does about every corporate reverse proxy — works today, because the stdlib carries <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">Authorization</code> when only the scheme changed. After this PR the token is stripped on that hop, and with <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">--follow-info-refs-redirect</code> on, <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">outgoingAuth</code> then withholds it from every follow-up RPC too. Result: a hard 401 on a config that works today, and the \\\"store it in the credential helper\\\" remedy doesn'\"'\"'t apply, because the host didn'\"'\"'t change.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">The stated goal is \\\"an https endpoint never leaks a token over plaintext http\\\" — that'\"'\"'s the <em>downgrade</em> direction only. Suggest allowing the upgrade:</p>\\n<div class=\\\"highlight highlight-source-go notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\\\" dir=\\\"a'"],"cwd":"file:///Users/puneetpalsingh/Documents/Code/OSSplayground/Entire/cli","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"python3 -c 'import sys;open(sys.argv[1],\"ab\").write(sys.argv[2].encode())' '/Users/puneetpalsingh/.codex/entire-20-audit-2026-11-16/public/git-sync-108.json' 'REDACTED\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\"><themed-picture data-catalyst-inline=\\\"true\\\"><picture><source media=\\\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\\\" srcset=\\\"https://camo.githubusercontent.com/a3745bd9c9e494e3d083228c1efdaf4ec2d519ad1c6adb4160d1c56c1ddeeb7b/68747470733a2f2f637572736f722e636f6d2f6173736574732f696d616765732f6669782d696e2d637572736f722d6461726b2e706e67\\\" data-canonical-src=\\\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/fix-in-cursor-dark.png\\\"><source media=\\\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\\\" srcset=\\\"https://camo.githubusercontent.com/8f892a2b1c7e36d49621daf12d83d3200c0ad6842ca1f6d8422631d06c99b3b2/68747470733a2f2f637572736f722e636f6d2f6173736574732f696d616765732f6669782d696e2d637572736f722d6c696768742e706e67\\\" data-canonical-src=\\\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/fix-in-cursor-light.png\\\"><img alt=\\\"Fix All in Cursor\\\" width=\\\"115\\\" height=\\\"28\\\" src=\\\"https://camo.githubusercontent.com/a3745bd9c9e494e3d083228c1efdaf4ec2d519ad1c6adb4160d1c56c1ddeeb7b/68747470733a2f2f637572736f722e636f6d2f6173736574732f696d616765732f6669782d696e2d637572736f722d6461726b2e706e67\\\" data-canonical-src=\\\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/fix-in-cursor-dark.png\\\"></picture></themed-picture></a></p>\\n\\n\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><sup>❌ Bugbot Autofix is OFF. To automatically fix reported issues with cloud agents, enable autofix in the <a href=\\\"https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">Cursor dashboard</a>.</sup></p>\\n\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><em>Comment <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">@cursor review</code> or <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">bugbot run</code> to trigger another review on this PR</em></p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><sup>Reviewed by <a href=\\\"https://cursor.com/bugbot\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">Cursor Bugbot</a> for commit <a class=\\\"commit-link\\\" data-hovercard-type=\\\"commit\\\" data-hovercard-url=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/b0e0f88541d3bbbe472233a7ce0bbe63124c4115/hovercard\\\" href=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/b0e0f88541d3bbbe472233a7ce0bbe63124c4115\\\"><tt>b0e0f88</tt></a>. Configure <a href=\\\"https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">here</a>.</sup></p>\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#discussion_r3813653044\",\n        \"body\": \"Confirmed and fixed in d3eb5b95 — and it was worse than the flag-token case, because it was **silent**.\\n\\nReproduced against httptest servers before fixing: with credentials only in the URL and no explicit `Auth`, the cross-site POST carried `Basic dXNlcjpVUkxTRUNSRVQ=` (`user:URLSECRET`). You had the mechanism exactly right — `resolvedEndpoint` was `*c.EndpointURL` with only scheme and host replaced, so userinfo came along, and net/http derives Basic auth from `req.URL.User`. `outgoingAuth` never sees that, so there was no warning either.\\n\\nBoth construction sites now go through one `setResolvedEndpoint` helper that drops userinfo when the result leaves the endpoint'\"'\"'s site, and warns when it does. Verified against the built binary: the redirect target now logs `Authorization=\\\"\\\"` and the operator gets the withheld-credentials warning.\\n\\nGood catch on the docs too — the claim that userinfo is bound like a flag token was aspirational when you flagged it, and is now true.\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 3813653044,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": 4972971969,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": 3813268165,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:00:59Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:00:59Z\",\n        \"review\": null,\n        \"side\": \"RIGHT\",\n        \"line\": 409,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": \"internal/gitproto/smarthttp.go\",\n        \"body_html\": \"<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Confirmed and fixed in <a class=\\\"commit-link\\\" data-hovercard-type=\\\"commit\\\" data-hovercard-url=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853/hovercard\\\" href=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853\\\"><tt>d3eb5b9</tt></a> — and it was worse than the flag-token case, because it was <strong>silent</strong>.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Reproduced against httptest servers before fixing: with credentials only in the URL and no explicit <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">Auth</code>, the cross-site POST carried <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">Basic dXNlcjpVUkxTRUNSRVQ=</code> (<code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">user:URLSECRET</code>). You had the mechanism exactly right — <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">resolvedEndpoint</code> was <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">*c.EndpointURL</code> with only scheme and host replaced, so userinfo came along, and net/http derives Basic auth from <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">req.URL.User</code>. <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">outgoingAuth</code> never sees that, so there was no warning either.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Both construction sites now go through one <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">setResolvedEndpoint</code> helper that drops userinfo when the result leaves the endpoint'\"'\"'s site, and warns when it does. Verified against the built binary: the redirect target now logs <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">Authorization=\\\"\\\"</code> and the operator gets the withheld-credentials warning.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Good catch on the docs too — the claim that userinfo is bound like a flag token was aspirational when you flagged it, and is now true.</p>\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#pullrequestreview-4972971969\",\n        \"body\": \"\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?u=54538e7ba4f0714d98f7a9289c5b91400b262152&v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 4972971969,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": null,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": null,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:00:59Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": null,\n        \"review\": \"COMMENTED\",\n        \"side\": null,\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": null,\n        \"body_html\": \"\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#discussion_r3813653319\",\n        \"body\": \"Fixed in d3eb5b95. You'\"'\"'re right, and this one breaks working setups rather than leaking anything — a false negative.\\n\\n`sameSite` now normalizes the default port per scheme before comparing, so `https://host` and `https://host:443` are the same site, as are `http://host` and `http://host:80`. The default port for the *other* scheme still counts as a real port (`https://host` vs `https://host:80` stays cross-site), and the subdomain case with an explicit default port is covered too. Five new cases in the table.\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 3813653319,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": 4972972271,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": 3813268177,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:01Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:02Z\",\n        \"review\": null,\n        \"side\": \"RIGHT\",\n        \"line\": 52,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": \"internal/gitproto/samesite.go\",\n        \"body_html\": \"<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Fixed in <a class=\\\"commit-link\\\" data-hovercard-type=\\\"commit\\\" data-hovercard-url=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853/hovercard\\\" href=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853\\\"><tt>d3eb5b9</tt></a>. You'\"'\"'re right, and this one breaks working setups rather than leaking anything — a false negative.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">sameSite</code> now normalizes the default port per scheme before comparing, so <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https://host</code> and <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https://host:443</code> are the same site, as are <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http://host</code> and <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http://host:80</code>. The default port for the <em>other</em> scheme still counts as a real port (<code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https://host</code> vs <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https://host:80</code> stays cross-site), and the subdomain case with an explicit default port is covered too. Five new cases in the table.</p>\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#pullrequestreview-4972972271\",\n        \"body\": \"\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?u=54538e7ba4f0714d98f7a9289c5b91400b262152&v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 4972972271,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": null,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": null,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:01Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": null,\n        \"review\": \"COMMENTED\",\n        \"side\": null,\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": null,\n        \"body_html\": \"\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#discussion_r3813653514\",\n        \"body\": \"Fixed in d3eb5b95. Verified: `syncer.newConn` had `if authMethod == nil { conn.CredentialHelper = ... }`, which is exactly the case where the token is *not* withheld — so a `--source-token` user hitting a cross-site redirect got a bare 401, and the remedy `docs/usage.md` recommends did not work.\\n\\nThe helper is now installed alongside an explicit token. For same-site requests `outgoingAuth` still returns the token, so `tryHelperRetry` returns early and the helper is never consulted — it only comes into play where the token is deliberately withheld, which is the behaviour the warning and docs describe. The warning also no longer promises a lookup unconditionally: it names the real next step depending on whether a helper is configured.\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 3813653514,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": 4972972543,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": 3813268184,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:03Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:03Z\",\n        \"review\": null,\n        \"side\": \"RIGHT\",\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": \"internal/gitproto/smarthttp.go\",\n        \"body_html\": \"<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Fixed in <a class=\\\"commit-link\\\" data-hovercard-type=\\\"commit\\\" data-hovercard-url=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853/hovercard\\\" href=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/d3eb5b95214037089e0fc3f465e1f09707689853\\\"><tt>d3eb5b9</tt></a>. Verified: <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">syncer.newConn</code> had <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">if authMethod == nil { conn.CredentialHelper = ... }</code>, which is exactly the case where the token is <em>not</em> withheld — so a <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">--source-token</code> user hitting a cross-site redirect got a bare 401, and the remedy <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">docs/usage.md</code> recommends did not work.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">The helper is now installed alongside an explicit token. For same-site requests <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">outgoingAuth</code> still returns the token, so <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">tryHelperRetry</code> returns early and the helper is never consulted — it only comes into play where the token is deliberately withheld, which is the behaviour the warning and docs describe. The warning also no longer promises a lookup unconditionally: it names the real next step depending on whether a helper is configured.</p>\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#pullrequestreview-4972972543\",\n        \"body\": \"\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?u=54538e7ba4f0714d98f7a9289c5b91400b262152&v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 4972972543,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": null,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": null,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:01:03Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": null,\n        \"review\": \"COMMENTED\",\n        \"side\": null,\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": null,\n        \"body_html\": \"\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#pullrequestreview-4973565789\",\n        \"body\": \"Real bug, right fix, and the `sameSite` test table covers the cases that matter. `go vet` and `go test ./...` pass on the branch. Two things I'\"'\"'d change before merge — both are false *negatives* (credentials withheld where they shouldn'\"'\"'t be), so they break working setups rather than leak anything.\\n\\n## 1. `http` → `https` on the same host is now treated as cross-site\\n\\n`samesite.go:43` requires an exact scheme match, so `sameSite(http://host, https://host)` is `false` (verified directly). A plain-`http` endpoint that redirects to `https` on the same host — GitHub does this, and so does about every corporate reverse proxy — works today, because the stdlib carries `Authorization` when only the scheme changed. After this PR the token is stripped on that hop, and with `--follow-info-refs-redirect` on, `outgoingAuth` then withholds it from every follow-up RPC too. Result: a hard 401 on a config that works today, and the \\\"store it in the credential helper\\\" remedy doesn'\"'\"'t apply, because the host didn'\"'\"'t change.\\n\\nThe stated goal is \\\"an https endpoint never leaks a token over plaintext http\\\" — that'\"'\"'s the *downgrade* direction only. Suggest allowing the upgrade:\\n\\n```go\\nif !strings.EqualFold(orig.Scheme, dest.Scheme) &&\\n\\t!(strings.EqualFold(orig.Scheme, \\\"http\\\") && strings.EqualFold(dest.Scheme, \\\"https\\\")) {\\n\\treturn false\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe port normalization already handles 80 → 443. Either way this belongs in the \\\"Behavior change to be aware of\\\" section, which currently mentions only non-same-site *hosts*.\\n\\n## 2. `guardRedirects` anchors on the user-typed endpoint forever\\n\\n`smarthttp.go:363` closes over `EndpointURL` at construction, but the connection'\"'\"'s effective endpoint moves — `setResolvedEndpoint` adopts the redirect host, and helper credentials are then looked up *for that host*. Any further redirect that never leaves the adopted host still fails `sameSite(EndpointURL, req.URL)` and loses its `Authorization`. Reproduced: endpoint 307s to host X, helper creds bound to X, X bounces `POST /repo.git/...` → `/final/repo.git/...`:\\n\\n```\\nGET  /repo.git/info/refs              Authorization=\\\"\\\"\\nPOST /repo.git/git-upload-pack        Authorization=\\\"Basic Z2l0OkhFTFBFUkNSRUQ=\\\"\\nPOST /final/repo.git/git-upload-pack  Authorization=\\\"\\\"      ← stripped on a same-host hop\\n```\\n\\nThat'\"'\"'s exactly the path the PR'\"'\"'s own documented remedy sends people down, so it undercuts the fix. The credential is bound to the host the *request* was aimed at, which the callback already has: use `via[0].URL` instead of the captured `endpoint`. That'\"'\"'s per-request accurate for the token case (identical when `requestURL() == EndpointURL`) and for the helper-retry case (`via[0].URL` is the challenge URL), and it lets the closure drop the captured endpoint entirely.\\n\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"nodo\",\n          \"name\": \"nodo\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/829178?u=2f501cf3464df66b7ec3a6ab1eafc90d41b97974&v=4\",\n          \"id\": 829178\n        },\n        \"id\": 4973565789,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": null,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": null,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T14:59:14Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": null,\n        \"review\": \"COMMENTED\",\n        \"side\": null,\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": null,\n        \"body_html\": \"<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Real bug, right fix, and the <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">sameSite</code> test table covers the cases that matter. <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">go vet</code> and <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">go test ./...</code> pass on the branch. Two things I'\"'\"'d change before merge — both are false <em>negatives</em> (credentials withheld where they shouldn'\"'\"'t be), so they break working setups rather than leak anything.</p>\\n<h2 dir=\\\"auto\\\">1. <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http</code> → <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https</code> on the same host is now treated as cross-site</h2>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">samesite.go:43</code> requires an exact scheme match, so <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">sameSite(http://host, https://host)</code> is <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">false</code> (verified directly). A plain-<code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http</code> endpoint that redirects to <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https</code> on the same host — GitHub does this, and so does about every corporate reverse proxy — works today, because the stdlib carries <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">Authorization</code> when only the scheme changed. After this PR the token is stripped on that hop, and with <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">--follow-info-refs-redirect</code> on, <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">outgoingAuth</code> then withholds it from every follow-up RPC too. Result: a hard 401 on a config that works today, and the \\\"store it in the credential helper\\\" remedy doesn'\"'\"'t apply, because the host didn'\"'\"'t change.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">The stated goal is \\\"an https endpoint never leaks a token over plaintext http\\\" — that'\"'\"'s the <em>downgrade</em> direction only. Suggest allowing the upgrade:</p>\\n<div class=\\\"highlight highlight-source-go notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\\\" dir=\\\"a'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":11833}},"started_at_ms":1794842143045,"completed_at_ms":1794842143046}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-16T15:15:43.176Z","ordinal":3112,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1d516-ad7f-7071-81ac-409a5dad90b1","turn_id":"01a1e4cf-42fc-7bf1-b6f5-3fd32ae90631","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-b2154f3a-ae44-493a-97d3-ccb72c7216bb","process_id":"55840","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","python3 -c 'import sys;open(sys.argv[1],\"ab\").write(sys.argv[2].encode())' '/Users/puneetpalsingh/.codex/entire-20-audit-2026-11-16/public/git-sync-108.json' 'uto\\\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\\\"if !strings.EqualFold(orig.Scheme, dest.Scheme) &amp;&amp;\\n\\t!(strings.EqualFold(orig.Scheme, &quot;http&quot;) &amp;&amp; strings.EqualFold(dest.Scheme, &quot;https&quot;)) {\\n\\treturn false\\n}\\\"><pre class=\\\"notranslate\\\"><span class=\\\"pl-k\\\">if</span> <span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">!</span><span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">strings</span>.<span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">EqualFold</span>(<span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">orig</span>.<span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">Scheme</span>, <span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">dest</span>.<span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">Scheme</span>) <span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">&amp;&amp;</span>\\n\\t<span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">!</span>(<span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">strings</span>.<span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">EqualFold</span>(<span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">orig</span>.<span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">Scheme</span>, <span class=\\\"pl-s\\\">\\\"http\\\"</span>) <span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">&amp;&amp;</span> <span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">strings</span>.<span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">EqualFold</span>(<span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">dest</span>.<span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">Scheme</span>, <span class=\\\"pl-s\\\">\\\"https\\\"</span>)) {\\n\\t<span class=\\\"pl-k\\\">return</span> <span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">false</span>\\n}</pre></div>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">The port normalization already handles 80 → 443. Either way this belongs in the \\\"Behavior change to be aware of\\\" section, which currently mentions only non-same-site <em>hosts</em>.</p>\\n<h2 dir=\\\"auto\\\">2. <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">guardRedirects</code> anchors on the user-typed endpoint forever</h2>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">smarthttp.go:363</code> closes over <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">EndpointURL</code> at construction, but the connection'\"'\"'s effective endpoint moves — <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">setResolvedEndpoint</code> adopts the redirect host, and helper credentials are then looked up <em>for that host</em>. Any further redirect that never leaves the adopted host still fails <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">sameSite(EndpointURL, req.URL)</code> and loses its <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">Authorization</code>. Reproduced: endpoint 307s to host X, helper creds bound to X, X bounces <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">POST /repo.git/...</code> → <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">/final/repo.git/...</code>:</p>\\n<div class=\\\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\\\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\\\"GET  /repo.git/info/refs              Authorization=&quot;&quot;\\nPOST /repo.git/git-upload-pack        Authorization=&quot;Basic Z2l0OkhFTFBFUkNSRUQ=&quot;\\nPOST /final/repo.git/git-upload-pack  Authorization=&quot;&quot;      ← stripped on a same-host hop\\\"><pre class=\\\"notranslate\\\"><code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">GET  /repo.git/info/refs              Authorization=\\\"\\\"\\nPOST /repo.git/git-upload-pack        Authorization=\\\"Basic Z2l0OkhFTFBFUkNSRUQ=\\\"\\nPOST /final/repo.git/git-upload-pack  Authorization=\\\"\\\"      ← stripped on a same-host hop\\n</code></pre></div>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">That'\"'\"'s exactly the path the PR'\"'\"'s own documented remedy sends people down, so it undercuts the fix. The credential is bound to the host the <em>request</em> was aimed at, which the callback already has: use <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">via[0].URL</code> instead of the captured <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">endpoint</code>. That'\"'\"'s per-request accurate for the token case (identical when <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">requestURL() == EndpointURL</code>) and for the helper-retry case (<code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">via[0].URL</code> is the challenge URL), and it lets the closure drop the captured endpoint entirely.</p>\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/108#issuecomment-5344059196\",\n        \"body\": \"Both fixed in 503ac1ba. You were right on both counts, and I verified each directly before changing anything — thank you, these were the two cases my own test table was blind to.\\n\\n## 1. `http` → `https` upgrade\\n\\nConfirmed: `sameSite(http://host, https://host)` was `false`, and so was `http://host:80 → https://host:443`. My stated rationale only justified blocking the *downgrade*, so the exact-match check was overreach.\\n\\nTook your suggestion, factored into a named helper so the asymmetry is explicit:\\n\\n```go\\nfunc schemeCompatible(src, dst string) bool {\\n\\tif src == dst { return true }\\n\\treturn src == \\\"http\\\" && dst == \\\"https\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nTest table gained five cases: the upgrade on the same host, the upgrade with default ports spelled out, the upgrade onto a subdomain, and — importantly — that the allowance can'\"'\"'t smuggle in a host or port change (`http://github.com` → `https://evil.example` and `http://host` → `https://host:8443` both stay cross-site).\\n\\nWorth noting the reasoning that convinced me it'\"'\"'s safe rather than just convenient: for an `http` endpoint the credential was already going to that host in the clear, so an upgrade strictly improves matters. There'\"'\"'s no configuration where withholding on an upgrade protects anything.\\n\\n## 2. `guardRedirects` anchoring\\n\\nConfirmed, and this was the more consequential one — it broke the exact remedy the PR documents. Reproduced your trace as a test, and verified it **fails** against the construction-time anchor:\\n\\n```\\n--- FAIL: TestGuardRedirectsAnchorsOnTheIssuedRequest\\n    credentials were stripped on a hop that never left the adopted host\\n```\\n\\n`via[0].URL` is the right anchor for the reason you give: it'\"'\"'s whatever `requestURL()` or the helper-retry target actually pointed at, so it'\"'\"'s accurate per request in both cases instead of describing a connection-level state that has since moved. The closure no longer captures the endpoint at all, and `guardRedirects` lost its `*url.URL` parameter as a result.\\n\\nOne addition: a nil anchor now fails closed. Go guarantees `len(via) >= 1` so it'\"'\"'s defensive only, but `sameSite(nil, x)` returning `false` means unknown provenance strips rather than forwards.\\n\\nA second test covers the other direction — a hop that genuinely leaves the issued request'\"'\"'s site still loses the header — so the two aren'\"'\"'t traded off against each other.\\n\\n## Docs\\n\\nBoth `docs/usage.md` and the changelog said \\\"same scheme and port\\\" and now describe the downgrade rule. I'\"'\"'ve also replaced the PR'\"'\"'s behavior-change section with an explicit table of what'\"'\"'s kept versus withheld, since the previous wording only mentioned hosts.\\n\\nRebased onto `main` now that #107 has merged. `gofmt`, `go vet`, `golangci-lint` (0 issues), `go test ./...` 18/18.\",\n        \"user\": {\n          \"login\": \"Soph\",\n          \"name\": \"Soph\",\n          \"email\": null,\n          \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70741?v=4\",\n          \"id\": 70741\n        },\n        \"id\": 5344059196,\n        \"pull_request_review_id\": null,\n        \"in_reply_to_id\": null,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-08-19T15:08:44Z\",\n        \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-19T15:08:44Z\",\n        \"review\": null,\n        \"side\": null,\n        \"line\": null,\n        \"start_line\": null,\n        \"path\": null,\n        \"body_html\": \"<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Both fixed in <a class=\\\"commit-link\\\" data-hovercard-type=\\\"commit\\\" data-hovercard-url=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/503ac1ba90d9be140bf6c4c348cc0b5b249eaf5d/hovercard\\\" href=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/commit/503ac1ba90d9be140bf6c4c348cc0b5b249eaf5d\\\"><tt>503ac1b</tt></a>. You were right on both counts, and I verified each directly before changing anything — thank you, these were the two cases my own test table was blind to.</p>\\n<h2 dir=\\\"auto\\\">1. <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http</code> → <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https</code> upgrade</h2>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Confirmed: <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">sameSite(http://host, https://host)</code> was <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">false</code>, and so was <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http://host:80 → https://host:443</code>. My stated rationale only justified blocking the <em>downgrade</em>, so the exact-match check was overreach.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Took your suggestion, factored into a named helper so the asymmetry is explicit:</p>\\n<div class=\\\"highlight highlight-source-go notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\\\" dir=\\\"auto\\\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\\\"func schemeCompatible(src, dst string) bool {\\n\\tif src == dst { return true }\\n\\treturn src == &quot;http&quot; &amp;&amp; dst == &quot;https&quot;\\n}\\\"><pre class=\\\"notranslate\\\"><span class=\\\"pl-k\\\">func</span> <span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">schemeCompatible</span>(<span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">src</span>, <span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">dst</span> <span class=\\\"pl-smi\\\">string</span>) <span class=\\\"pl-smi\\\">bool</span> {\\n\\t<span class=\\\"pl-k\\\">if</span> <span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">src</span> <span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">==</span> <span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">dst</span> { <span class=\\\"pl-k\\\">return</span> <span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">true</span> }\\n\\t<span class=\\\"pl-k\\\">return</span> <span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">src</span> <span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">==</span> <span class=\\\"pl-s\\\">\\\"http\\\"</span> <span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">&amp;&amp;</span> <span class=\\\"pl-s1\\\">dst</span> <span class=\\\"pl-c1\\\">==</span> <span class=\\\"pl-s\\\">\\\"https\\\"</span>\\n}</pre></div>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Test table gained five cases: the upgrade on the same host, the upgrade with default ports spelled out, the upgrade onto a subdomain, and — importantly — that the allowance can'\"'\"'t smuggle in a host or port change (<code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http://github.com</code> → <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https://evil.example</code> and <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http://host</code> → <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">https://host:8443</code> both stay cross-site).</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Worth noting the reasoning that convinced me it'\"'\"'s safe rather than just convenient: for an <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">http</code> endpoint the credential was already going to that host in the clear, so an upgrade strictly improves matters. There'\"'\"'s no configuration where withholding on an upgrade protects anything.</p>\\n<h2 dir=\\\"auto\\\">2. <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">guardRedirects</code> anchoring</h2>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Confirmed, and this was the more consequential one — it broke the exact remedy the PR documents. Reproduced your trace as a test, and verified it <strong>fails</strong> against the construction-time anchor:</p>\\n<div class=\\\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\\\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\\\"--- FAIL: TestGuardRedirectsAnchorsOnTheIssuedRequest\\n    credentials were stripped on a hop that never left the adopted host\\\"><pre class=\\\"notranslate\\\"><code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">--- FAIL: TestGuardRedirectsAnchorsOnTheIssuedRequest\\n    credentials were stripped on a hop that never left the adopted host\\n</code></pre></div>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\"><code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">via[0].URL</code> is the right anchor for the reason you give: it'\"'\"'s whatever <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">requestURL()</code> or the helper-retry target actually pointed at, so it'\"'\"'s accurate per request in both cases instead of describing a connection-level state that has since moved. The closure no longer captures the endpoint at all, and <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">guardRedirects</code> lost its <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">*url.URL</code> parameter as a result.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">One addition: a nil anchor now fails closed. Go guarantees <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">len(via) &gt;= 1</code> so it'\"'\"'s defensive only, but <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">sameSite(nil, x)</code> returning <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">false</code> means unknown provenance strips rather than forwards.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">A second test covers the other direction — a hop that genuinely leaves the issued request'\"'\"'s site still loses the header — so the two aren'\"'\"'t traded off against each other.</p>\\n<h2 dir=\\\"auto\\\">Docs</h2>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Both <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">docs/usage.md</code> and the changelog said \\\"same scheme and port\\\" and now describe the downgrade rule. I'\"'\"'ve also replaced the PR'\"'\"'s behavior-change section with an explicit table of what'\"'\"'s kept versus withheld, since the previous wording only mentioned hosts.</p>\\n<p dir=\\\"auto\\\">Rebased onto <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">main</code> now that <a class=\\\"issue-link js-issue-link\\\" data-error-text=\\\"Failed to load title\\\" data-id=\\\"5192270519\\\" data-permission-text=\\\"Title is private\\\" data-url=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/issues/107\\\" data-hovercard-type=\\\"pull_request\\\" data-hovercard-url=\\\"/entireio/git-sync/pull/107/hovercard\\\" href=\\\"https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/107\\\">#107</a> has merged. <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">gofmt</code>, <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">go vet</code>, <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">golangci-lint</code> (0 issues), <code class=\\\"notranslate\\\">go test ./...</code> 18/18.</p>\"\n      